\paragraph{A central regularity requirement.} For the source metric, inversion of the $(v,r)$ block gives $g^{rr}=a$. Define the spherical mass function by $m(v,r)=r(1-a(v,r))/2$. Trapping $a<0$ at a sphere is therefore exactly $2m/r>1$ there. At a smooth regular spherical center, the areal radius agrees to first order with proper radial distance and the radial metric in regular local coordinates has expansion $1+O(r^2)$. Under this regular-center hypothesis $a=1+O(r^2)$, hence
\[
 m(v,r)=O(r^3)\qquad(r\downarrow0).
\]
Thus proving failure of this bound along an approaching central region would obstruct that regular-center geometry.

\paragraph{Trapping at a positive radius is insufficient by itself.} Smooth nonnegative mass profiles can satisfy $m(r)=cr^3$ near zero and $2m(r_0)/r_0>1$ at a positive radius. For example $m(r)=cr^3$ up to $r_0$ has the latter property when $2cr_0^2>1$. This is only a mass-profile example, not asserted to solve the Einstein--Vlasov constraints or evolution; it shows that the algebraic trapped-sphere inequality does not imply a central failure of the cubic bound at that time.

